  "answer_to_user": "Here are big, strong companies that carry very little debt. \"Low debt\" here means a low debt-to-equity ratio — the company owes far less than the value of what its shareholders own. I also required above-average profitability so these are solid, not just debt-light. Examples: Nvidia (NVDA, debt/equity ~0.06), Microsoft (MSFT, ~0.13), Alphabet/Google (GOOG, ~0.18), Palantir (PLTR, ~0.02), Micron (MU, ~0.06), Costco (COST, ~0.24), Lam Research (LRCX) and Arista Networks (ANET). Why low debt matters for safety: a company with little debt has smaller fixed interest bills, so it's far less likely to run into trouble in a downturn or when interest rates rise — it isn't relying on borrowed money to stay afloat. That said, low debt does NOT equal guaranteed safety — the business can still stumble, and the stock can still fall. This is for research, not a purchase recommendation."
}